# Trivy misconfiguration exceptions — dev environment.
# Each entry is an accepted risk with rationale, not a blanket suppression.
# Prod-profile hardening (envs/prod) closes several of these; see notes.

# Unrestricted egress (0.0.0.0/0) on the ALB and service security groups.
# Fargate tasks need egress to AWS APIs (ECR, SSM, CloudWatch, Secrets Manager),
# to RDS/Redis, and — for bridge — to external IdPs (Google/Entra). Scoping
# egress would require VPC endpoints for every service. The design is explicit
# in modules/network/main.tf. Deferred to prod hardening.
AWS-0104

# ECR image tags are mutable. Intentional: release.yml re-points the ':dev' tag
# on every build (see envs/dev/main.tf). Version tags (:vX.Y.Z) are immutable by
# convention. Prod would set IMMUTABLE and drop the rolling ':dev' tag.
AWS-0031

# Public subnets auto-assign public IPs. The cheap dev profile (enable_nat=false)
# runs tasks in public subnets for egress without a NAT gateway — documented in
# modules/network/main.tf. Data services stay in private subnets. Prod uses
# private subnets + NAT.
AWS-0164

# SNS topic is not encrypted. It carries only CloudWatch alarm metadata
# ("CPU high"), which is non-sensitive, and AWS-managed-key SSE complicates
# CloudWatch->SNS delivery (the key policy can't grant cloudwatch.amazonaws.com).
# CMK-backed SNS is prod hardening.
AWS-0095

# ALB is internet-facing. That is the point — it fronts the public IdP, SSO,
# portal, and console. `drop_invalid_header_fields` is always on; WAFv2
# attaches in the prod profile only (`count = local.cheap ? 0 : 1`).
AWS-0053

# S3 buckets (ALB access logs, site) use SSE-S3 (AES256) rather than a customer
# managed key. Access-log records carry request lines, so "non-sensitive" has to
# be earned rather than asserted: portal's one-time tokens are mailed in the URL
# fragment and never reach a request line — bar links mailed before the switch,
# which carry ?token= until the 24h verify-email TTL lapses — and bridge's
# ?code= is spent inside the request that carries it. What lands here is IPs,
# paths and user agents.
# CMK adds key-management cost and is deferred to prod hardening.
AWS-0132
